{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And no man put his ornament upon him.</b> There is a difficulty: since of their own accord they did not put their ornaments on themselves, what is this that He went back and said, <b>And now, put off your ornament from upon you</b>? And it appears to me that there were here two kinds of ornament: the first is ordinary ornament — ring and nose-ring and jewelry with which they would adorn themselves. The second is those crowns that were given to them at Chorev on account of their saying <b>We will do and we will hear</b>. And the first time, when they heard this thing that the Holy One, blessed be He, said — <b>for I will not go up in your midst</b> — and behold they were like ones ostracized from heaven, therefore <b>they mourned and no man put his ornament upon him</b>. From the fact that it says <b>his ornament</b> (עֶדְיוֹ) and does not say <b>the children of Israel</b>, as is said afterward — <b>So the children of Israel stripped themselves</b> — infer from it that it speaks of the ornament particular to each and every man according to his wealth: the rich man would have much and the poor man little. And they accepted repentance upon themselves on account of the sin that resulted from the ornament, as is said, <b>And all the people broke off the golden rings</b> etc. But those crowns that they received at Chorev they did not break off, for it would have appeared to them like casting off the yoke of the Torah, as if they said: we have no portion in the Torah and in its crown. Therefore it was said to them a second time, <b>And now, put off your ornament from upon you</b> — even those crowns; and concerning this it is said, <b>So the children of Israel stripped themselves of their ornament from Mount Chorev</b> — that ornament given to them at Mount Chorev. And here it says <b>the children of Israel</b> and does not say <b>his ornament</b> (עֶדְיוֹ), because all Israel were equal in it, the rich like the poor." "And Maharai explained that a person has many kinds of ornament, and some of them were on them and some were not on them. And at first they did not put on themselves ornaments that were not yet on them, but those that were already on them they did not take off. Therefore it says, 'And now, put off your ornament' — even this that is already upon you. And also according to our interpretation it is well settled why the language of taking down (הוֹרָדָה) is mentioned the second time and not the first — because the ornament of Mount Chorev was constantly upon them, which is not so with ordinary ornament."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {0 "The word וַיִּתְנַצְּלוּ is rendered 'stripped themselves' following Rashi on Exodus 33:6; the verb can also mean 'were delivered/spared.'", 1 "Maharai (מַהֲרִי״א) appears to refer to Rabbi Joseph Bekhor Shor or another early Rishon; the acronym is left as found."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:25:03.877846333Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}